          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does \"code first\" mean, and what actually goes wrong if the two are sequenced the other way round?",
          "answer": "**What \"code first\" means:** The corpus describes \"code first\" and \"use additional code\" as a pair that fixes sequencing. The underlying condition is reported first, and its manifestation (the way it shows up in a particular body system) is reported second. \"Code also\" means two codes are needed but neither has to come first. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.) The passages I retrieved do not quote the exact standard wording of these instructions, so I can't give it word for word.\n\n**What goes wrong when the order is reversed:** The retrieved passages do not describe this. They do not cover misclassification, errors in attributing cause or manifestation, or claim and data-quality problems. I can't explain those consequences from this corpus, so please check a coding manual or a qualified medical coder for them.\n\nThe corpus does show why the sequence matters. The diabetes document explains the \"with\" convention: when the index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification presumes a relationship unless the record says the conditions are unrelated. So a record listing type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease is coded as diabetic kidney disease by default. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.) Coding rules like these determine which condition is treated as the cause, so getting them wrong can change how the record reads.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
